# Q4 first failing probe — cross-composite reaction.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The simplest expression of the Q4 problem: two sibling sub-composites
# `alice` and `bob`, each holding a single-qubit `QuantumSystem`. We
# WANT to express a reaction that entangles them — CNOT on qubits
# living in different composites. The full vision: reactor detects the
# cross-composite redex, emits a merge intent (see
# `docs/merge-protocol.md`), the merge fuses alice+bob into one
# composite holding their tensor-product state, and the reaction's
# reactum applies a CNOT inside the merged composite.
#
# THIS FILE IS WHAT DOESN'T WORK YET. It runs (doesn't error), but
# the output shows the three concrete blockers documented in
# `docs/merge-protocol.md` "First-probe findings":
#
#   1. Composite-output leak. `alice` / `bob`'s emitted state is
#      nested several levels deep (`alice.state.state...`) and
#      includes schema descriptors (`_type: "Any"`). Bridge tap is
#      not projecting to the slot value only.
#   2. `any`-typed slot accumulation. The internal `Hold` process
#      tries to "just re-emit state each tick" to make the bridge
#      pick it up, but with `overwrite[any]` semantics the value
#      ends up being SUMMED per tick (4× initial at --time 2).
#   3. No sibling-addressing syntax. A reaction expressed at the Lab
#      level can't refer to `alice.state` and `bob.state` directly —
#      `^.foo` is parent, `%.foo` is self, no `<sibling>.foo`.
#
# Until 1 and 2 are fixed, the matcher can't see what's there
# cleanly. Once they are, the reaction definer (slice 4 in
# `docs/merge-protocol.md`) becomes implementable.
#
# Run: `chrysalis run quantum-cross-cnot.ys --time 1`
# Inspect the output to see the leak + accumulation pattern.

# ── Minimal `QuantumSystem` composite — holds a state on its bridge.
# `state0` is a PARAM (initial value at instantiation); the internal
# `Hold` process re-writes state every tick so the bridge tap captures
# it. (BLOCKER 2: under `any` typing the re-emit accumulates instead
# of overwriting.)
process Hold ~{state :: any} ->{state :: overwrite[any]}
(
  {state: state}
)

composite QuantumSystem[state0 :: any = {'0': 1.0, '1': 0.0}]
  ~{state :: any @ state = state0}
  ->{state :: any @ state}
(
  state: state0 |
  hold:  Hold ~{state: state} ->{state: state}
)

# ── Two sibling QuantumSystems in a typed map. Following the
# `environment.ys` pattern: `map[QuantumSystem]` triggers per-entry
# composite instantiation; each entry has its own bridge.
# (BLOCKER 1: the resulting `systems` output is deeply nested with
# schema descriptors, not a clean `{alice: {state: ...}, bob: {state: ...}}`.)
composite Lab ~{} ->{
  systems :: map[QuantumSystem] @ systems
} (
  systems: {
    alice: QuantumSystem[state0: {'0': 0.7071, '1': 0.7071}]
      ~{state: %.state} ->{state: %.state},
    bob: QuantumSystem[state0: {'0': 1.0, '1': 0.0}]
      ~{state: %.state} ->{state: %.state}
  }
)

# ── The reaction we WANT to express (commented out — not parseable
# today; BLOCKER 3 is the sibling-addressing syntax).
#
# reaction Entangle =
#   alice ~{state: a} | bob ~{state: b}
#   ->
#   merged ~{state: cnot(tensor(a, b))}
#
# brs: BRS[reactions: [Entangle]] ~{state: systems} ->{state: systems}
